<task id="084" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>Sanitize the filename at every remaining SPE upload site, consolidate the eight sanitizers onto one, and make an unsanitized site a build failure</title>
    <phase>0c Secure Documents — Wave 2</phase>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Touches thirteen `.cs` files across `Api/`, `Services/`, `Workers/` and `Infrastructure/Graph/`, one of which (`ChatWordExportEndpoints`) is client-controlled path injection into SharePoint Embedded. Adds an ArchTest rule and restores a deleted service plus its DI registration. Tags include `bff-api` and `security`, and the change modifies `tests/**` — TEST-MODIFYING override applies regardless.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Two judgment calls that a mechanical pass gets wrong. (1) `Api/OBOEndpoints.cs` must NOT be blanket-sanitized — its `{*path}` is a wildcard route where a caller may legitimately supply a sub-path, so the answer there is per-segment REJECTION, not rewriting; deciding that requires reading the route contract rather than pattern-matching. (2) Restoring `MessageAttachmentMaterializer` means separating the two things the deletion was RIGHT about (folder-minting path, pre-076 caller-authoritative drive) from the one it was wrong about (the caller-count reasoning), and restoring only the hardened form.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <effort-reason>Brownfield root-cause work whose deliverable is completeness across a surface that has been miscounted at every previous attempt. The site list supplied at kickoff was short by three (ComposeService, DocumentsEndpoints, SpeAdminGraphService) and the duplicate-sanitizer count was short by four.</effort-reason>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Edits the same files as 083 (`DocumentsEndpoints.cs`, both Chat endpoint files) and the same two ArchTest guards. Two agents rewriting one upload surface produce a silent merge mess.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>075, 076, 083</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, api, security, testing, dataverse</tags>
    <estimated-effort>6-10 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Owner directive 2026-08-29, following commit `3c3d4072a`. That commit found the root cause of the mystery SPE folders — an unsanitized filename becomes the upload path verbatim — and fixed it at the ONE site where it had been observed (the Office add-in's free-text "Document Name" box). It explicitly did not sweep the rest. The same root cause was latent at every other site, most sharply at `ChatWordExportEndpoints`, whose path is `request.Filename` straight off the request body. The same directive corrected an earlier instruction of the owner's own: the deletion of `MessageAttachmentMaterializer` in that commit was made on a production-only caller search and is reversed here.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>A file name IS the whole SPE upload path. Graph creates every '/'-delimited segment of an upload path as a folder, implicitly, as a side effect of the write. Removing hardcoded folder PREFIXES did not subsume this — it fixed the half the server wrote. Fix the half the USER writes, everywhere, and make the next unsanitized site fail the build. Then restore MessageAttachmentMaterializer, improved.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE security engineer. The deliverable is a closed set plus a forcing function: after this task no server-constructed SPE upload path can carry a user-supplied separator, there is exactly ONE sanitizer, and a new site that forgets is a red build rather than a folder in an operator's SPE Admin view.</role>

  <goal>Every site that puts a filename into an SPE upload path sanitizes it through one shared implementation. The seven private duplicate sanitizers are consolidated onto it. `Api/OBOEndpoints.cs` is REVIEWED rather than blanket-sanitized, and its per-segment validation is hardened without losing its intentional sub-path capability. `MessageAttachmentMaterializer` is restored with a flat path, a sanitized filename, and its caller-supplied `DriveId` still demoted to a resolver fallback. An ArchTest rule makes an unsanitized site a build failure, with negative and positive controls.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>⭐ **READ `git show 3c3d4072a` FIRST.** Its commit message carries the evidence, the Graph mechanics, and the trap.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§1 — THE MECHANIC, STATED EXACTLY
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`SpeFileStore.UploadSmallAsync` resolves to
`graphClient.Drives[id].Root.ItemWithPath(path).Content.PutAsync(...)`. Graph creates **every**
`'/'`-delimited segment of `path` as a FOLDER. There is no "file name" concept on this API — the name
IS the path.

**The production incident.** The Word add-in's "Document Name" box is free text. A user typed a date:
`New Word Document from Word Web Add In 8/24/2026`. Graph minted folder `…Add In 8` containing folder
`24` containing an extension-less file `2026`. Because the upload is app-only (MI), SPE Admin showed no
human creator, which is what made it look externally created.

**Why the 2026-08-28 flattening did not cover this.** That change deleted folder PREFIXES — `'/'`
characters the SERVER wrote into a path literal. This `'/'` comes from a USER, through a value the
literal treats as opaque (`$"{id:N}_{fileName}"`). The flat-path ArchTest rule was green throughout.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§2 — THE SITE LIST. Derive it; do not inherit it.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The kickoff brief named ten sites and cited `SpeUploadPathIsFlatGuardTests.cs` as enumerating eleven.
**Neither is a list.** That guard is a regex SCANNER with a `>= 5` non-vacuity floor; it contains no
site enumeration. The usable starting point is the DISCOVERED INVENTORY comment in
`SpeWriteSinkContainerProvenanceGuardTests.cs` plus `git show 3c3d4072a --stat`, and both must be
re-derived rather than trusted — three sites were missing from the brief:

  · `Services/Compose/ComposeService.cs` — `ResolveFileName(request.DisplayName)`, **client-supplied**
  · `Api/DocumentsEndpoints.cs` — `fileName` is a **QUERY-STRING** parameter, and the route writes app-only
  · `Infrastructure/Graph/SpeAdminGraphService.cs` — a SECOND facade, also calling `ItemWithPath(fileName)`,
    which the flat-path guard does not probe because it scans for different sink names

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§3 — ONE SANITIZER (root CLAUDE.md §11)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The brief reported "four private duplicates". There were **seven**, in two genuinely different classes:

*Same function, consolidate*: `EmlGenerationService`, `MessagingArchiver`, `GraphMessageToEmlConverter`
— all strip-invalid-chars-then-truncate-50, differing only in truncation and empty-fallback.

*Different function, leave and say why*: `DocumentsBulkEndpoints.SanitizeZipEntryName` /
`.SanitizeFilenameSegment`, `ReviewMemoEndpoints.BuildMemoFileName`, `DocxExportService` /
`PdfExportService.GenerateFileName` — these REPLACE with `_` or `-` rather than strip, and their output
becomes a `Content-Disposition` header or a ZIP entry name, never an SPE path.

⚠️ `GraphMessageToEmlConverter`'s copy was a **latent production bug**, not merely a duplicate: it used a
bare `Path.GetInvalidFileNameChars()`, which on the **linux-x64** runtime this publishes to returns only
`{'\0','/'}`. Its unit test asserted `<`, `>`, `"` were stripped and passed — because it runs on Windows,
where that call returns the wide set.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§4 — `Api/OBOEndpoints.cs` IS DIFFERENT. Review it; do not sanitize it.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`PUT /api/obo/containers/{id}/files/{*path}` is a wildcard route where the caller may legitimately
address a location inside a container it already holds. Rewriting a caller's path would move their bytes
without telling them, so the answer is per-segment **rejection**, not sanitization. `ValidatePathForOBO`
already blocks `..`, control characters, a trailing `/`, blank, and `>1024` chars. Establish what it does
NOT block, and harden that without removing the sub-path capability.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§5 — RESTORING `MessageAttachmentMaterializer`
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Deleted in `3c3d4072a` on a **production-only** caller search. The type also had a DI registration, five
unit tests including the CHAT-ATTACHMENT-POLICY 25 MB + MIME gate, an ArchTest allow-list citation, and
live consumers in two sibling worktrees. Restore it IMPROVED — the deletion was right about the
folder-minting path and about the PRE-076 caller-authoritative `DriveId`, and both stay fixed. Task 076
had already demoted `request.DriveId` to the fallback ARGUMENT of
`CommunicationContainerResolver.ResolveContainerAsync`; restoring it in any caller-authoritative form is
the defect class this project exists to close.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§6 — THE FORCING FUNCTION
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

An inventory goes stale the day someone adds a site. Extend
`tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/SpeUploadPathIsFlatGuardTests.cs` with a rule that inspects the path ARGUMENT at
every sink call, not the literals near it. Two halves, and they are load-bearing together: the argument
must be a LOCAL whose name ends in `path` (so no site can pass `fileName` straight through and slip past
a name-keyed scan — a real blind spot, at `ComposeService` and `EmailAttachmentProcessor`), AND that
local's initializer must run through the one sanitizer. Negative and positive controls both required per
`tests/CLAUDE.md`; the positive control must include the REAL TREE.</background>
