  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Establish the ArchTest baseline on a CLEAN tree and record which tests fail, with their assertion text. `ADR-010`'s message carries a count (153 → 156) that must be unchanged afterwards.</step>
    <step order="1">DERIVE the site list from the sinks, not from any prose inventory: every call to `UploadSmallAsync` / `UploadSmallAsUserAsync` / `CreateUploadSessionAsUserAsync`, plus any other facade reaching `ItemWithPath`. For each, walk backwards to where the filename came from and classify it: client-supplied, attacker-influenced (an inbound email's attachment name), or server-generated.</step>
    <step order="2">Find every sanitizer in the tree. Separate the ones that are the SAME function from the ones that merely look similar, and say which is which. Move the surviving implementation to a home every layer can reach without an inverted dependency, and delete the duplicates.</step>
    <step order="3">Sanitize each site. Where an id carries the collision-uniqueness, sanitize INSIDE the composition (`$"{id:N}_{Sanitize(name)}"`) — folding an id in front does not protect a slash-bearing name.</step>
    <step order="4">Review `ValidatePathForOBO` per §4. Determine empirically whether the sub-path capability has any client caller, and report it.</step>
    <step order="5">Restore `MessageAttachmentMaterializer` per §5: the class, its DI registration, its five unit tests, and its ArchTest declaration. Record at the class WHY it was deleted and restored, so the reasoning is not repeated.</step>
    <step order="6">Fix the stale doc comments claiming `HostContext` drives container resolution (`ChatWordExportEndpoints`, `ChatDocumentEndpoints`). Verify the claim against `ResolveContainerId` before rewriting it.</step>
    <step order="7">Extend the ArchTest guard per §6, and the integration tests with the REAL failing string as the input.</step>
    <step order="8">PERTURBATION-CHECK both new instruments: remove sanitization at one site and confirm the ArchTest rule AND the site's behavioural test redden; restore. A guard nobody has seen fail is a guard nobody knows works.</step>
    <step order="9">Build (0 warnings), targeted tests, full unit suite, ArchTests vs the step-0 baseline, publish size, CVE scan.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If restoring `MessageAttachmentMaterializer`'s `DriveId` in a non-caller-authoritative form proves impossible, STOP. Reinstating a caller-chosen container is worse than leaving the class deleted.</trigger>
    <trigger>If `ValidatePathForOBO` turns out to be load-bearing for a live client that genuinely sends multi-segment paths, STOP before changing its contract — hardening it would then be a breaking change to a shipped route.</trigger>
    <trigger>If the sanitizer cannot be placed anywhere reachable without an inverted layer dependency, STOP rather than duplicating it. A second sanitizer is how this codebase got seven.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`OfficeEmailEnricher.SanitizeFileName` is the one PUBLIC sanitizer and already carries the widened Windows-strict character set (`3c3d4072a`). `SpeUploadPathIsFlatGuardTests` provides the guard harness. `CommunicationContainerResolver` provides the record-aware container decision. `SpeFlatUploadPathTests` provides the fake SPE drive that reproduces the path-keyed replace.</existing>
    <extension>The sanitizer is MOVED, not re-authored, and seven duplicates collapse onto it — the change is net-negative in implementation count. The new ArchTest rule extends an existing guard class rather than adding one. No new service, no new endpoint, no new package, no publish-size delta.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>`POST /api/ai/chat/export/word` takes `request.Filename` off the request body straight into the SPE upload path: client-controlled path injection, mitigated by nothing (the `.docx` check constrains only the suffix — `a/b/c.docx` passes it). `PUT /api/drives/{driveId}/upload` does the same from a query string, app-only. Inbound email attachment names reach two more. Every one of them creates folders in a customer's container that no one asked for and that no later permission change can tidy — and the incident that started this was a user typing a DATE, not an attack.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">One shared sanitizer; seven duplicates consolidated or classified as genuinely different with a written reason</output>
    <output type="code">Every filename→path site sanitized, id-carrying compositions sanitized inside the hole</output>
    <output type="code">`ValidatePathForOBO` hardened per-segment, sub-path capability intact</output>
    <output type="code">`MessageAttachmentMaterializer` restored — flat path, sanitized name, `DriveId` still a fallback — with its DI registration, tests, and ArchTest declaration</output>
    <output type="code">Stale `HostContext`-drives-container doc comments corrected</output>
    <output type="test">ArchTest rule 2 + negative and positive controls incl. the real tree</output>
    <output type="test">Behavioural tests using the real failing string, one per distinct mechanism</output>
    <output type="doc">The derived site list, the sanitizer census, and the `ValidatePathForOBO` finding</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>No site puts an unsanitized filename into an SPE upload path. Proven by the source rule, not by an inventory.</criterion>
    <criterion>Exactly one sanitizer implementation exists for SPE upload paths. Every remaining `Path.GetInvalidFileNameChars()` site is documented as serving a different purpose (Content-Disposition, ZIP entry) and never reaches an upload path.</criterion>
    <criterion>`Api/OBOEndpoints.cs` is NOT blanket-sanitized. `ValidatePathForOBO` rejects a leading `/`, empty segments, `.`/`..` segments, and invalid characters per segment, while a legitimate multi-segment path still succeeds.</criterion>
    <criterion>`MessageAttachmentMaterializer` exists, is registered in DI, has its five unit tests passing, and its upload path contains no `/`. `request.DriveId` is passed as the resolver's fallback argument and is never consulted ahead of the record.</criterion>
    <criterion>The ArchTest rule fires on an unsanitized site (negative control), does not fire on the sanctioned shape OR on the real production tree (positive control), and its non-vacuity assertion pins the number of inspected sink calls.</criterion>
    <criterion>The perturbation check is performed and its result recorded: removing sanitization at one site reddens both the source rule and that site's behavioural test.</criterion>
    <criterion>The sink-provenance guard's declarations are updated for the restored site, and no rule is weakened.</criterion>
    <criterion>Build 0 warnings; full unit suite at or above its prior pass count; ArchTests show the SAME pre-existing failures as the step-0 baseline and no new ones; publish size reported with the five-field convention; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>The number in every brief for this project has been wrong at least once. This one arrived saying ten sites, eleven in a guard that contains no list, and four duplicate sanitizers; the real figures were fourteen sink calls, a regex scanner, and seven duplicates — and the task number itself (086) was already occupied by `086-access-event-log-schema`, which is the second filing collision in this project's history (see the 080–083 → 086–089 renumbering note in TASK-INDEX). Re-derive every count. The deeper lesson this task encodes is narrower and more useful than "check your counts": a production-caller count of zero is not a deletion licence when a DI registration, a policy-gate test suite, an ArchTest citation, or a sibling worktree depends on the type.</notes>
</task>
